course: history
lesson: 2
title: The first farms and towns
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: About 12,000 years ago some people stopped following the animals and started planting seeds and keeping animals near home: they became farmers.
explain: Nobody invented farming in one go. It grew slowly, and it began separately in several places: the Middle East, China, Mexico, the Andes and others.
explain: Think of a vegetable patch at school: once you plant beans you cannot wander off, you have to stay, water them and guard them. Farmers did the same, so they built houses and villages that stayed put.
explain: When farms grew more food than the farmers needed, not everyone had to farm. Some people became potters, builders, priests or leaders, and villages grew into towns.
explain: Later people learned to melt copper out of rocks and to mix it with tin to make bronze, so historians name these times after the best tool material: the Stone, Bronze and Iron Ages.
fact: About 12,000 years ago people in the Middle East began planting wheat and barley and keeping sheep and goats.
fact: Farming also started separately in China, Mexico, the Andes and other places; it was not invented by one person in one place.
fact: Dogs were the first tamed animals, long before farming; sheep, goats, cattle and pigs came later, and horses much later.
fact: Farmers built houses of mud brick or wood near their fields and lived in villages all year round.
fact: Settled people made pots of baked clay to store grain, water and oil.
fact: Çatalhöyük in Turkey was a town about 9,000 years ago with houses so tightly packed that people walked over the roofs and climbed in through holes with ladders.
fact: Jericho, near the river Jordan, is one of the oldest towns in the world; over 10,000 years ago its people built a stone wall and a tall tower.
fact: Stonehenge in England is a great circle of huge stones raised about 4,500 years ago by farming people using ropes, sledges and wooden rollers.
fact: The stones of Stonehenge line up with the sunrise in midsummer and the sunset in midwinter.
fact: When farms made more food than needed, some people could have other jobs, such as potter, builder, priest or leader.
fact: People learned to melt copper from rocks, and later mixed copper with tin to make bronze, which is harder.
fact: Historians name early times after the best tool material: the Stone Age, then the Bronze Age, then the Iron Age.
fact: The Ages did not start everywhere at the same time; some places used iron much earlier than others.
exercise: About how long ago did farming begin in the Middle East? | About 12,000 years ago | About 1,200 years ago | About 120 years ago | About 1 million years ago
exercise: Which crops did the first Middle Eastern farmers plant? | Wheat and barley | Potatoes and tomatoes | Rice and green tea | Corn and cocoa beans
exercise: Where else did farming begin on its own? | In China, Mexico and the Andes | Only in England | Nowhere, it spread from one village | Only on islands in the Pacific
exercise: Which animal was tamed first, long before farming? | The dog | The horse | The house cat | The camel
exercise: Why did farmers build houses that stayed in one place? | They had to look after their fields and animals | They had forgotten how to walk far over the hills | Kings ordered everyone to stay home | Nobody had found any animals left to hunt anywhere
exercise: What were the first villages' houses usually made of? | Mud brick or wood | Steel and glass | Ice blocks | Concrete and plastic
exercise: What were clay pots used for? | Storing grain, water and oil | Cooking on electric stoves | Sending messages to other towns | Building the walls of houses
exercise: How did people at Çatalhöyük get into their houses? | Through holes in the roof with ladders | Through big wooden front doors on the street | Through tunnels under the ground | Through windows on the side
exercise: What did the people of Jericho build over 10,000 years ago? | A stone wall and a tall tower | A pyramid and a sphinx | A wooden castle with a deep moat | A bridge over the sea
exercise: Who built Stonehenge? | Farming people with ropes and sledges | Giants who came down from the far north | Aliens who came from another planet | A wizard named Merlin
exercise: About how old is Stonehenge? | About 4,500 years old | About 450 years old | About 45,000 years old | About 45 years old
exercise: What do the stones of Stonehenge line up with? | The midsummer sunrise and the midwinter sunset | The North Star, which they point at every night of the year | The river Thames | The road to London
exercise: Why could some people become potters or priests? | Farms grew more food than the farmers needed | Hunting had become against the law | Everyone was rich from selling gold | Farming was too boring for them
exercise: What is bronze made of? | Copper mixed with tin | Iron mixed with sand | Gold mixed with silver | Clay mixed with stone
exercise: In which order did the three Ages come? | Stone, then Bronze, then Iron | Iron, then Bronze, then Stone | Bronze, then Stone, then Iron | Stone, then Iron, then Bronze
exercise: Did the Iron Age begin everywhere on the same day? | No, some places used iron much earlier | Yes, all over the world in one year | Yes, on the first day of the year 1000 | No, it began only in America
test: Chloe reckons one clever bloke invented farming and told everyone. True or nah? | No, farming grew slowly in several places | Yes, a king in Egypt invented it and told the world | Yes, and he wrote a book about it | No, farming was brought by aliens
test: A town grows so much food that half the people do not need to farm. What happens? | Some people take other jobs like potter or builder | Everyone stops eating so much | The extra food is thrown into the sea | All the people move back to hunting
test: If farmers had no pots, what problem would they have? | Storing grain and water would be hard | They could not plant seeds | They could not walk to the fields | Their dogs would run away
test: A documentary says aliens built Stonehenge. What do historians say? | Farmers moved the stones with ropes, sledges and rollers | Giants carried the stones one by one from Wales on their backs | The stones grew there by themselves | Romans built it as a temple
test: Why would a stone wall around Jericho be useful? | To protect the town and its stored food | To keep the sea out of the town | To hold up the roofs of the houses | To mark a football pitch
test: dad says bronze came before stone tools lol. is he right? | No, stone tools came long before bronze | Yes, bronze is the oldest material people used | Yes, people started with metal | No, iron came before both
test: Why did the people of Stonehenge care about midsummer and midwinter? | The seasons mattered for farming and festivals | They used the stones as a clock for school | They needed to know when to fly | The stones told them when the bus came
test: Two villages have dogs, sheep and cattle. Which animal did their great-great ancestors tame first, before farming? | The dog | The sheep | The cattle | The horse
test: Why is bronze better than copper for tools? | Bronze is harder | Bronze is softer | Bronze is lighter than air | Bronze never needs to be made
test: Houses packed so tight you walk on roofs, about 9,000 years ago. Which town? | Çatalhöyük in Turkey | Jericho by the Jordan | Stonehenge in England | Uruk in Iraq
test: Why is it fair to say farming changed people's lives more than almost anything? | It let people settle, build towns and take new jobs | It made everyone stop eating meat | It ended all wars for ever | It meant nobody had to work hard any more, as food grew itself
exam: Identify the region in which, approximately twelve millennia ago, the cultivation of wheat and barley is first attested. | The Middle East | Northern and Western Europe | The southern tip of Africa | North America and Canada
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the origins of agriculture? | It arose gradually and independently in several regions | It was devised by a single individual in one locality | It was introduced simultaneously throughout the world | It originated exclusively in the British Isles
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the composition of bronze. | An alloy of copper and tin | An alloy of iron and carbon | An alloy of gold and silver | A compound of clay and stone
exam: It is given that a surplus of food was produced by early farming communities. Determine the principal consequence thereof. | Certain persons were enabled to pursue occupations other than farming | All persons were obliged to resume hunting | The population was obliged to diminish | Commerce was thereupon prohibited
